Z||000005||ThatRickGuy||06-08-2001||10:19 AM||||Ya know, I've gone to these guys like 5 or 6 times, for things I either didn't have a tool to do, or no time, or what ever, and their usually really good. But the last 2 times (the bushings and the timing) The old manager is gone (and the new one wont tell me why) and I found out the fiero friendly mechanic left too. Definately time to find a new shop.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||06-11-2001||03:40 PM||||Hey, just thought I'd post an update. I got the car back into this shop this morning, manager said he'd call my cell phone as soon as he had news. Well, after lunch, arround 1:00, I finally called him. He said the car was on the hoist and he'd get back to me in a few minutes when they figured it out. So, at 3:00 I called back again. First thing he said was "You need to go back to stock." after some questioning he told me the bushings were too short, and that they were the wrong ones. I explained to him that I bought those bushing from a store that sells nothing but fiero parts and it is highly unlikely that I got the wrong part, but he insisted. So, I said 'If these are the wrong parts, and you installed them, and there are obviously wrong, why didn't you call me immediatly to get the correct parts in?' he sluffed the question off, claiming something about the engine torque changing them. so I asked if they would cover the labor since they made a mistake, and he said 'no, once you bring in your own parts, labor is not warentied', even though the old manager had specifically told me that they would stand behind the labor, but if the parts broke, it was on me. So, in another 30 minutes I get to go argue w/ the owner. It's really too bad, this used to be a really good shop, I've recommended a lot a business to them. So anyways, if you wind up in the Quantico, VA area, do not goto the Quantico Service Station. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||06-11-2001||03:57 PM||||A gap between the poly and the outer sleeve? In other words the bushing diameter is too small? Sounds like they may have put the upper bushings in the lower or something???<P>For the up and down play in boot on the rack. If it's on the passenger side, order a bushing from Rodney Dickman. His are aluminum, factory are plastic and bad about wearing out. <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||ThatRickGuy||06-11-2001||04:06 PM||||can't be the uppers, I installed them myself, a perfect fit. I only had them do the lowers because I dodn't have a spring compressor and I needed the lower ball joints replaced anyways. Thanks for the pointer on the rack bushing, didn't even think about it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ThatRickGuy||06-11-2001||06:32 PM||||Okay, back from the shop. After about 30 minutes w/ the owner and arguing over wether or not his mechanic should have realized the 1/8 inch gap is not a good thing, and the fact that their police is no warenty on 'performance' parts, he offered to do the labor over for 1/2 price, $146. Plus they'll check my timing again (which they set too far retarded) and tighten the altinator belt again. that leaves my with another $60+ and shipping to cover the bushings. now that I have access to a spring compressor I think I'll just do it myself though. <P>Anyways, I do have a question, we were trying to figure out where this 1/8th inch game came from, so I got him to attempt to measure the bushings, the smaller-longer part came in at a hair under 1.5in diameter in the middle, and the larger-short part came in at just over 2in diameter anyone happen to have easy access to a set of bushings, stock or polys that can be measured? I need to make sure these are the wrong ones before I order another set and find out my a-arms are screwed. Thanks!<p>[This message has been edited by ThatRickGuy (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
